%I A003192 M1369 #81 Nov 27 2026 12:24:18
%S A003192 0,0,2,5,10,17,24,35,47,61,76,94,113,135
%N A003192 Length of uncrossed knight's path on an n X n board.
%C A003192 I used ZDD techniques to show that a(9)=47. (This is the longest uncrossed knight's path on a 9 X 9 board; thus I confirmed the conjecture that the paths of length 47, found by hand long ago, are indeed optimum.) - _Don Knuth_, Jun 24 2010
%C A003192 For best known results see link to Alex Chernov's site. - _Dmitry Kamenetsky_, Mar 02 2021
%C A003192 a(10)-a(14) were computed by a row-by-row transfer ("profile") dynamic program. Each two-row knight move is cut at its midpoint, so that all pieces of the path between two consecutive row lines are segments whose left-to-right order must be preserved (an idea of Derek Kisman). The values 61, 76, 94, 113 and 135 coincide with the lengths of the longest paths found previously, which are thereby proved optimal: 61, 76, 94 and 113 by Robin Merson in 1990 and 135 by Alexander Fischer in 2006 (see the Jelliss link); Chernov's database also credits paths of all five lengths to Bernard Lemaire, 1976-1980. - _Jeff Sponaugle_, Nov 25 2026
%D A003192 D. E. Knuth, Long and skinny knight's tours, in Selected Papers on Fun and Games, CSLI, Stanford, CA, 2010. (CSLI Lecture Notes, vol. 192)
%D A003192 J. S. Madachy, Letter to N. J. A. Sloane, Apr 26 1975.
